\subsection*{3.2 Sum of the Measures of the Exterior Angles of a Polygon}

On many occasions a knowledge of exterior angles may throw light on the nature of interior angles and sides.

\section*{DO THIS}

Draw a polygon on the floor, using a piece of chalk. (In the figure, a pentagon ABCDE is shown) (Fig 3.2).
We want to know the total measure of angles, i.e, \(m \angle 1+m \angle 2+m \angle 3+m \angle 4+m \angle 5\). Start at A. Walk along \(\overline{\mathrm{AB}}\). On reaching B , you need to turn through an angle of \(m \angle 1\), to walk along. \(\overline{\mathrm{BC}}\) When you reach at C , you need to turn through an angle of \(m \angle 2\) to walk along \(\overline{\mathrm{CD}}\). You continue to move in this manner, until you return to side AB . You would have in fact made one complete turn.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/2dba4960-d233-4c45-9dbe-6e0a59dba658-037.jpg?height=505&width=607&top_left_y=582&top_left_x=1253)

Therefore, \(m \angle 1+m \angle 2+m \angle 3+m \angle 4+m \angle 5=360^{\circ}\).
This is true whatever be the number of sides of the polygon.
Therefore, the sum of the measures of the external angles of any polygon is \(360^{\circ}\).

