\subsection*{5.4 Finding the Square of a Number}

Squares of small numbers like \(3,4,5,6,7, \ldots\) etc. are easy to find. But can we find the square of 23 so quickly?
The answer is not so easy and we may need to multiply 23 by 23 .
There is a way to find this without having to multiply \(23 \times 23\).
We know
\[23=20+3\]

Therefore
\[\begin{aligned}
23^{2} & =(20+3)^{2}=20(20+3)+3(20+3) \\
& =20^{2}+20 \times 3+3 \times 20+3^{2} \\
& =400+60+60+9=529
\end{aligned}\]

