\subsection*{8.4 Multiplying a Monomial by a Polynomial}

Expression that contains two terms is called a binomial. An expression containing three terms is a trinomial and so on. In general, an expression containing, one or more terms with non-zero coefficient (with variables having non negative integers as exponents) is called a polynomial.

\subsection*{8.4.1 Multiplying a monomial by a binomial}

Let us multiply the monomial \(3 x\) by the binomial \(5 y+2\), i.e., find \(3 x \times(5 y+2)=\) ? Recall that \(3 x\) and \((5 y+2)\) represent numbers. Therefore, using the distributive law, \(3 x \times(5 y+2)=(3 x \times 5 y)+(3 x \times 2)=15 x y+6 x\)

We commonly use distributive law in our calculations. For example:
\[\begin{array}{rlr}
7 \times 106 & =7 \times(100+6) & \\
& =7 \times 100+7 \times 6 & \\
& =700+42=742 & \\
7 \times 38 & =7 \times(40-2) & \\
& =7 \times 40-7 \times 2 & \\
& =280-14=266 &
\end{array}\]

Similarly,
\[(-3 x) \times(-5 y+2)=(-3 x) \times(-5 y)+(-3 x) \times(2)=15 x y-6 x\]
and
\(5 x y \times\left(y^{2}+3\right)=\left(5 x y \times y^{2}\right)+(5 x y \times 3)=5 x y^{3}+15 x y\).
What about a binomial × monomial? For example, \((5 y+2) \times 3 x=\) ?
We may use commutative law as : \(7 \times 3=3 \times 7\); or in general \(a \times b=b \times a\)
Similarly,
\[(5 y+2) \times 3 x=3 x \times(5 y+2)=15 x y+6 x \text { as before. }\]

