\subsection*{9.6 Volume and Capacity}

There is not much difference between these two words.
(a) Volume refers to the amount of space occupied by an object.
(b) Capacity refers to the quantity that a container holds.

Note: If a water tin holds \(100 \mathrm{~cm}^{3}\) of water then the capacity of the water tin is \(100 \mathrm{~cm}^{3}\). Capacity is also measured in terms of litres. The relation between litre and \(\mathrm{cm}^{3}\) is, \(1 \mathrm{~mL}=1 \mathrm{~cm}^{3}, 1 \mathrm{~L}=1000 \mathrm{~cm}^{3}\). Thus, \(1 \mathrm{~m}^{3}=1000000 \mathrm{~cm}^{3}=1000 \mathrm{~L}\).

Example 8: Find the height of a cuboid whose volume is \(275 \mathrm{~cm}^{3}\) and base area is \(25 \mathrm{~cm}^{2}\).

Solution:
\[\begin{aligned}
\text { Volume of a cuboid } & =\text { Base area × Height } \\
\text { Hence height of the cuboid } & =\frac{\text { Volume of cuboid }}{\text { Base area }} \\
& =\frac{275}{25}=11 \mathrm{~cm}
\end{aligned}\]

Height of the cuboid is 11 cm .
Example 9: A godown is in the form of a cuboid of measures \(60 \mathrm{~m} \times 40 \mathrm{~m} \times 30 \mathrm{~m}\). How many cuboidal boxes can be stored in it if the volume of one box is \(0.8 \mathrm{~m}^{3}\) ?
Solution:
\[\begin{aligned}
& \text { Volume of one box }=0.8 \mathrm{~m}^{3} \\
& \text { Volume of godown }=60 \times 40 \times 30=72000 \mathrm{~m}^{3}
\end{aligned}\]

Number of boxes that can be stored in the godown \(=\frac{\text { Volume of the godown }}{\text { Volume of one box }}\)
\[=\frac{60 \times 40 \times 30}{0.8}=90,000\]

Hence the number of cuboidal boxes that can be stored in the godown is 90,000 .
