\section*{1. More about Pythagorean triplets}

We have seen one way of writing pythagorean triplets as \(2 m, m^{2}-1, m^{2}+1\).
A pythagorean triplet \(a, b, c\) means \(a^{2}+b^{2}=c^{2}\). If we use two natural numbers \(m\) and \(n(m>n)\), and take \(a=m^{2}-n^{2}, b=2 m n, c=m^{2}+n^{2}\), then we can see that \(c^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}\).
Thus for different values of \(m\) and \(n\) with \(m>n\) we can generate natural numbers \(a, b, c\) such that they form Pythagorean triplets.
For example: Take, \(m=2, n=1\).
Then, \(a=m^{2}-n^{2}=3, b=2 m n=4, c=m^{2}+n^{2}=5\), is a Pythagorean triplet. (Check it!)
For, \(m=3, n=2\), we get,
\(a=5, b=12, c=13\) which is again a Pythagorean triplet.
Take some more values for \(m\) and \(n\) and generate more such triplets.
